  "summary": "Four studies directly apply federated learning to sepsis early warning tasks. Iyer et al. [D0063] predict sepsis onset before clinical recognition using federated learning on ICU vital signs from the IEEE-CIS dataset, reporting an accuracy of 0.91. Brennan et al. [D0062] address early detection of septic deterioration in intensive care with federated learning on ICU vital signs from the MIMIC-IV dataset, reporting a MAPE of 0.868. Haddad et al. [D0061] also predict sepsis onset before clinical recognition with federated learning on ICU vital signs, using the HateCheck dataset and reporting an AUROC of 0.672. Okafor et al. [D0064] tackle the same prediction task using privacy-preserving training across institutions (a federated paradigm) on ICU vital signs from the SentinelYield-2 dataset, achieving an F1 of 0.726. Silva et al. [D0069] address sepsis onset prediction with learned node embeddings on ICU vital signs from the HateCheck dataset, reporting an F1 of 0.946, though this study uses graph-based rather than federated methods.",
